  "manualA11yB1": "A click on an on-screen keyboard presses and releases the key at once, so holding does not work with it: the recording stops right away. Use “Tap or hold” instead: a short press starts the recording, and the next press ends it.",
  "manualA11yB2": "We recommend this setting for an on-screen keyboard, or if holding is hard for you. You find it under Settings → Keyboard shortcuts.",
  "manualA11yB3": "The floating button no longer moves when you click it with a slightly unsteady hand. It is only dragged once you move it clearly.",
  "manualA11yHeading": "On-screen keyboard and limited hand movement",
  "manualC01S1B1": "On first start, WhisPaste takes you through seven steps of setup. The dots and the step count at the top show where you are. Each step has one main action, and you can go back at any time. Everything can be changed later in Settings.",
  "manualC01S1B2About": "About you: your first name (optional), what you use WhisPaste for and how you found it. Each question has the equal answer “Prefer not to say”; “Next” turns on once you have chosen an answer. The answers reach us only anonymously, with the usage counts. If you chose “Prefer not to say”, a card under “Get involved” in the Overview asks once more after about ten dictations; “Don't ask again” closes it for good.",
  "manualC01S1B2I1": "Start: an overview and the speech model. To pick another one, use the link “Other speech model”. The download starts only after you agree.",
  "manualC01S1B2I2": "Access: the permission that lets WhisPaste paste text automatically. The steps depend on your system. Once the permission works, WhisPaste switches to “Copy & Auto-Paste”.",
  "manualC01S1B2I3": "Microphone: choose the input device and watch the level to check that WhisPaste hears you. Without speech, Next stays off; you can always skip the test.",
  "manualC01S1B2I4": "Hotkey: set the key combination that starts a recording. If another app already uses it, you can finish setup only after you choose a different one.",
  "manualC01S1B2I5": "First dictation: speak into the practice chat, and the text appears there in the window. While you speak, the chat shows that WhisPaste is listening; while the text is being made, it shows that it is being processed. “Try later” skips the exercise.",
  "manualC01S1B2I6": "Privacy and start: the privacy switches, what happens with the text after transcription, and the main action “Start WhisPaste”. If you skipped the access page, it says “Copy to clipboard” there; “Change” takes you back to the access page, where you set up Auto-Paste. Below it says how long your texts stay in Recordings.",
  "manualC01S1B3": "Pausing setup: if you close the window during setup, reopen it from the tray icon or the menu bar with “Resume setup”.",
  "manualC01S1B3Panel": "The tray panel: on macOS and Windows a click on the tray or menu bar icon opens a small panel; on Linux you open it with “Open panel”, the first entry in the tray menu. At the top are the status and a button that starts or stops a dictation, below them your last three recordings, quick switches for microphone, language (your favorites), profile and Smart Mode, plus “Open WhisPaste” and “Settings”. Rarely used commands and “Quit” sit behind “…”. Tab and the arrow keys select an entry, Enter triggers it. The panel does not take the focus away from the app you are writing in, so the text still lands there. Escape or a click outside closes it. A right click shows the plain menu. If the panel cannot open, the plain menu appears instead.",
  "manualC01S1B4": "Existing users: after an update, WhisPaste shows a hint that opens the microphone page as a check. Your settings stay unchanged.",
  "manualC01S1B5": "First steps: after setup, the start page shows a card with three steps: a first dictation pasted into another app, a dictation longer than 30 seconds, and your own terms in the dictionary. A step is ticked only when it really happened. You can close the card at any time; under Settings, in General, “Show again” brings it back.",
  "manualC01S1Heading": "Setup on first start",
  "manualC01S2B1": "WhisPaste recommends a device with at least {ram} of memory (RAM). On devices with less, WhisPaste still starts and recommends a compact speech model. If too little memory is free for a larger model, WhisPaste asks before loading it.",
  "@manualC01S2B1": {
    "placeholders": {
      "ram": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC01S2B2": "You need a microphone to record. In onboarding you can continue without one, but you can only record once a microphone is connected.",
  "manualC01S2B3": "On the Mac, WhisPaste runs only on Apple Silicon (M1 or newer) with macOS 13 Ventura or later; Intel Macs are not supported.",
  "manualC01S2Heading": "System requirements",
  "manualC01S3B1": "WhisPaste turns your speech into text, either on your device or through a cloud provider with your own key. On your device it needs a model. You start the download yourself; it does not start automatically.",
  "manualC01S3B2I1": "Parakeet: fast, for European languages, about {k1}.",
  "@manualC01S3B2I1": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC01S3B2I2": "Whisper: all 99 languages, models from about {k1} to about {k2}.",
  "@manualC01S3B2I2": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k2": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC01S3B2I3": "The models are stored in the app’s data folder, in the {k1} subfolder.",
  "@manualC01S3B2I3": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC01S3B3": "In the Speech recognition area, the entry “Re-run benchmark” measures how fast each installed tier runs on your computer. The results show on the model cards how much longer a tier takes and determine the recommended Whisper tier. The recommendation is the most accurate model that runs fluently: the largest Whisper tier if your computer carries it, otherwise Parakeet if it writes your language. For English it is Parakeet. The test does not start during a recording. In the model setup view, WhisPaste first checks speed on weak or unknown hardware using a bundled speech clip. It downloads the compact model if needed. The test then takes at most 20 seconds and can be cancelled. Larger tiers are estimated; cancellation or failure keeps the hardware recommendation. Strong hardware skips this short test.",
  "manualC01S3B4": "A Whisper model stays loaded after a recording, so the next start is faster. Under “Keep model loaded” in the speech recognition section you choose: Always (default) saves waiting time but needs the memory all the time. Automatic keeps the model loaded when your computer has enough memory, otherwise it unloads after 5 minutes. After idle time unloads it after the time you set. When memory runs short, WhisPaste unloads the model automatically and loads it again once there is room.",
  "manualC01S3B5": "If the selected speech model is missing, the dock shows “Speech model missing”. The dock’s speech menu then offers it as its first entry to download, for Whisper and Parakeet alike. Pressing the shortcut without a model shows a notice with “Download now”. When you switch to Whisper in Settings and the selected Whisper model is missing, WhisPaste picks one that is already downloaded.",
  "@manualC01S3B5": {"description": "Manual, chapter model download: what the dock, the shortcut and the engine switch do when the selected speech model is missing (UIA-MDL-GATE b, UIA-XP-7-04)."},
  "manualC01S3B6": "Mac only (macOS 26 and later): Apple speech recognition is available as a third engine. It uses the language you chose in Settings, never the system language. The system downloads its language model; you start the download in the engine's row. The first-run setup also offers it as a card next to the recommended model; “Next” waits until the system has downloaded the language. If it cannot process a recording, does not know the language or takes too long, Whisper transcribes the same recording and a short notice tells you. Windows and Linux do not show this option because it does not exist there.",
  "manualC01S3Heading": "Downloading a speech model",
  "manualC01Title": "Getting started",
  "manualC02S1B1": "The hotkey is a key combination that works in every program. Default: {k2} on Windows and Linux, {k3} on Mac. You can change it under Settings → Keyboard shortcuts. Map a free key to F13, then press it here. F13–F24 work without modifiers on Windows and Linux; macOS supports up to F20. Hold and double-tap work like other dictation keys. On Wayland, the desktop must accept the shortcut through its global shortcuts portal; hold and double-tap need release events. If unavailable, use a desktop shortcut for Start/Stop.",
  "@manualC02S1B1": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k2": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k3": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC02S1B2": "If another program already uses the combination, WhisPaste uses {k2} instead for the hotkey (on Mac, {k3}). The other hotkeys stay off until you set a free combination.",
  "@manualC02S1B2": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k2": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k3": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC02S1Heading": "The hotkey",
  "manualC02S2B1": "In Settings → Keyboard shortcuts, choose how the hotkey works:",
  "manualC02S2B2I1": "“Hold” (default for new installs): hold the hotkey, speak, and release. Releasing ends the recording.",
  "manualC02S2B2I2": "“Press to start and stop”: one press starts the recording, a second press ends it.",
  "manualC02S2B2I3": "“Tap or hold”: a short press starts and ends the recording, holding records until you release. The threshold is under “Advanced” at the end of the page.",
  "manualC02S2Heading": "Hold or toggle",
  "manualC02S3B1": "The dock at the bottom of the main window shows the current state. While recording, the main button changes to “Stop recording”, and the status area shows the current step. The dock is always there and cannot be turned off.",
  "manualC02S3B2I1": "“Recording overlay” set to “Floating (always visible)”: a small waveform overlay above all programs. Default: off.",
  "manualC02S3B2I2": "“Floating button”: a button you click to record. Default: off. With “Slim capsule when idle”, it rests as a narrow capsule and opens to the full pill while you hover over it. Under Linux with Wayland the capsule stays narrow, and the desktop may limit its position and whether it stays on top.",
  "manualC02S3B2I3": "“Overlay size” offers Normal, Compact, Mini and Nano. Nano is 88 × 24 pixels and shows only a short wave and the status glyph, with no text and no timer. You can also change the size with a right-click on the overlay. The “Glass” background is almost colourless: on macOS 26 and Windows 11 the system's own glass shows through, elsewhere a faint tint. “Wave style” draws your voice as Bars, Line, Dots, Ribbons or Pulse. Ribbons and Pulse show your voice flowing in; they move only with the level, also when motion is reduced. Mini and Nano show no timer or cancel symbol, and Nano no live transcript; those switches are then greyed out and the choice is kept for the other sizes. The live transcript works with Whisper and, on macOS 26 or later, with Apple speech recognition (which needs a fixed language); it is not available with Parakeet. An interim text never replaces the final transcript.",
  "manualC02S3B2I4": "When the microphone is missing, fails to start, loses its signal, your voice arrives very quietly, or the duration limit is near, the overlay grows into a small notice card with fitting buttons. Turn the quiet-voice notice off with “Quiet microphone hint”. Over a running recording the cross only hides the card (“Hide hint”); Esc asks first before the recording is discarded. After a confirmed paste the overlay names the target app, for example Slack. Pure status and success notices close on their own after a reading time (4 seconds plus 40 milliseconds per character, at least 8 seconds); errors, warnings and every notice with a button stay until you close them, and every card has a close cross. A footer line on each card names the keys that work. It always names the trigger you set, and never \"finish and paste\" without a running recording. A notice that the app is still busy goes away as soon as it is ready again. While your text is still being processed, it names only the cancel key, which discards it; if you press the hotkey then, a short hint tells you the text is on its way. By keyboard, on a card with a button and on a card that stays until you close it, outside a running recording, the cancel key (Esc or F12) closes the card and the other key presses the first button, or the second when you press it twice in quick succession, without taking focus from your app. A card that closes itself and has no button leaves both keys to your app. The keys are claimed only while the card stands. The extend button on the card extends the limit once and the recording keeps running; the click is never a stop, and the card stays for 3 seconds past the limit so a late click still counts. When you edit a selection with AI, a card first shows the result as a preview: Enter or “Apply” replaces the selection, Esc or “Discard” leaves it unchanged, and holding the edit hotkey again refines the result with a further instruction.",
  "manualC02S3B3": "You choose the microphone in the dock’s “More” menu, which also shows the current device. In a narrow window, the dock moves more controls there, including the hotkey, the Auto-Paste hint, the update notice, and “Thanks”. Everything stays reachable there.",
  "manualC02S3Heading": "Seeing the recording",
  "manualC02S4B1": "Under “After transcription”, choose what happens after each recording:",
  "manualC02S4B2I1": "“Copy to clipboard” (default): the text goes to the clipboard, and you paste it yourself. After the first dictation copied this way, a notification shows once and leads to this setting.",
  "manualC02S4B2I2": "“Auto-Paste at cursor”: WhisPaste pastes the text at the cursor.",
  "manualC02S4B2I3": "“Copy & Auto-Paste”: both.",
  "manualC02S4B2I4": "“Do nothing”: WhisPaste neither copies nor pastes the text.",
  "manualC02S4B3": "Apps on the “Auto-Paste blocklist” get no automatic pasting. The text then goes to the clipboard, or only to Recordings if “Copy to the clipboard when apps are blocked” is off. Enter one app per line in the field. If a paste fails, WhisPaste shows a notice and the text stays on the clipboard.",
  "manualC02S4B4": "On Windows, auto-paste does not work in programs running with administrator rights.",
  "manualC02S4B5": "On Linux, WhisPaste marks the text with a hint that only KDE's Klipper honours. Other clipboard managers such as GPaste or CopyQ also store your transcripts: turn off their clipboard recording or delete the entry there. On Linux with Wayland, WhisPaste reports errors only by notification and tray.",
  "@manualC02S4B5": {
    "description": "Linux clipboard history: only KDE Klipper honours the hint; other clipboard managers still record transcripts."
  },
  "manualC02S4B6": "After pasting, WhisPaste normally puts back what was on your clipboard before. Under “Clipboard after pasting” you can choose “Keep the transcript” instead: then the transcript stays on the clipboard. If a paste cannot be confirmed in time, the transcript stays there, unless you switch on “Restore even without confirmation”. If WhisPaste cannot write the clipboard receipt, a calm notice says: Paste not confirmed – the text is on the clipboard. If macOS denies access to the clipboard, WhisPaste cannot keep the previous content and does not restore it; the transcript then stays on the clipboard. Once, a hint shows the way to System Settings.",
  "manualC02S4B7": "If a text lands in the wrong field, switch on the “Paste last dictation again” shortcut under “Keyboard shortcuts”. It pastes the text of your last recording again into the app in front. It is set to Ctrl+Cmd+V on the Mac and Alt+Shift+V on Windows and Linux. With saving recordings off, WhisPaste only knows the last recording since it started. During a recording the shortcut does nothing. If there is nothing to paste yet, the overlay says so briefly.",
  "@manualC02S4B7": {"description": "Manual: the optional hotkey that pastes the last dictation again."},
  "manualC02S4B8": "The “Clipboard Quick-Paste panel” opens at the screen edge with your recordings, snippets and clipboard history. A click pastes an entry. You can also drag an entry straight into another app. On Linux there is no running history: WhisPaste only picks up the clipboard when you open the panel. In Settings → Appearance you can exclude apps such as password managers; their copies normally do not enter the history.",
  "@manualC02S4B8": {"description": "Manual: the quick-paste side panel; dragging entries out works on macOS and Windows, not yet on Linux."},
  "manualC02S4B9": "In the middle of a sentence, WhisPaste fits the start of your dictation: after an unfinished sentence, function words such as articles and conjunctions start in lower case, after a full stop the text starts with a capital, and the space in between is right. Names and dictionary terms stay as they are, and Recordings always keep your original text. If the field cannot be read, the text goes in unchanged. You can turn this off with “Fit the start of a dictation to the text” in Text & insertion. The version from the Mac App Store does not have this, because its sandbox cannot read other apps' fields.",
  "manualC02S4Heading": "Pasting after recording",
  "manualC02Title": "Recording and pasting",
  "manualC03S1B1": "The quick note is a note that WhisPaste appends new recordings to. This lets you collect thoughts or a list without creating a new note each time.",
  "manualC03S1B2": "If no note is marked yet, WhisPaste creates a new one the first time and marks it.",
  "manualC03S1Heading": "What the quick note does",
  "manualC03S2B1I1": "Open the note in the Notes area and turn on “Make this the quick note the hotkey appends to”. “Quick note — remove the mark” removes the mark.",
  "manualC03S2B1I2": "Turn on the quick-note hotkey under Settings → Keyboard shortcuts. It is off by default. The key is {k1} ({k2}, or {k3} on Mac).",
  "@manualC03S2B1I2": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k2": {
        "type": "String"
      },
