  },
  "manualC03S2Heading": "Setting it up",
  "manualC03Title": "Quick note",
  "manualC04S1B1": "{k1} edits the recognized text before it is pasted. It is off by default; you turn it on yourself.",
  "@manualC04S1B1": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC04S1B2": "By default, processing runs on your device (“Locally on device”). With “OpenAI”, the text is sent to OpenAI. The privacy chapter has the details.",
  "manualC04S1Heading": "What Smart Mode does",
  "manualC04S2B1": "A preset decides what happens to the text:",
  "manualC04S2B2I1": "“Off”: no post-processing; the text stays as it was recognized.",
  "manualC04S2B2I2": "“Cleanup”: removes filler words such as “um” and fixes punctuation and capitalization, without changing the wording, meaning, or language.",
  "manualC04S2B2I3": "“Concise”: removes repetition and filler, keeps every important fact and the language.",
  "manualC04S2B2I4": "“Translate”: translates the text into the target language you set.",
  "manualC04S2B2I5": "“Your presets”: on the Smart Mode page, “New preset” creates a preset with a name and your own instruction, such as “Write it as a polite e-mail”. It is then offered wherever you choose a preset. Names, numbers, dates and links stay unchanged. An instruction works most reliably with one task and a clear target format; it can be at most 500 characters long. Longer presets from earlier versions keep running unchanged and can only be saved again once shortened.",
  "manualC04S2B3": "After a dictation, Recordings shows what Smart Mode changed: “Compare before and after” puts the recognized text next to the result. From there you can switch back to the recognized version.",
  "manualC04S2Heading": "Presets",
  "manualC04S3B1": "For “Translate”, choose a target language: German, English, Spanish, French, Portuguese, Mandarin, or Russian. The default is English.",
  "manualC04S3B2": "A separate hotkey for {k1} is off by default. It uses the preset and target language from the settings.",
  "@manualC04S3B2": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC04S3Heading": "Target language and hotkey",
  "manualC04S4B1": "The local variant downloads a text model of about {size}. If your device has less than {ram} of RAM, WhisPaste warns before the download, but you can still download. While running, the text model needs an extra 4 to 5 GB of RAM.",
  "@manualC04S4B1": {
    "placeholders": {
      "size": {"type": "String"},
      "ram": {"type": "String"}
    }
  },
  "manualC04S4B2": "While no text model is set up (local model not downloaded, or no OpenAI key for OpenAI), all presets except “Off” are locked. Locked presets carry a lock. Under the preset picker, the reason sits next to a button that downloads the text model right away or leads to the key; clicking a locked preset jumps to the “Download” button. The dock menu names the reason as well. Once the model is downloaded, the presets can be picked at once, without a restart. If post-processing still fails, WhisPaste says so and tells you whether the unedited text was pasted or is only on the clipboard.",
  "manualC04S4Heading": "Resources",
  "manualC04S5B1": "After a selection edit, the notice offers “Undo”. If nothing has happened in between, WhisPaste presses Cmd+Z (macOS) or Ctrl+Z (Windows, Linux) in the app where the text was replaced, so that app’s own undo restores the original. This works the same on macOS, Windows and Linux (X11).",
  "manualC04S5B2": "If WhisPaste cannot be sure the keystroke lands in the right place, it sends nothing: you typed or clicked elsewhere since, another window is in front, the app is a terminal or not known as a text app, or the system does not allow it (Linux with Wayland). Then “Undo” copies the original text to the clipboard instead, and the notice says so. Paste it yourself; the previous clipboard content is replaced.",
  "manualC04S5Heading": "Undoing an edit of selected text",
  "manualC04Title": "Smart Mode",
  "manualC05S1B1": "A replacement automatically swaps certain words or phrases for other text in every recording.",
  "manualC05S1B2": "Each replacement has one or more trigger phrases, the phrases to look for, and the new text. Enter several trigger phrases for the same text under “Trigger phrases”.",
  "manualC05S1B3": "For symbols such as brackets, @ or %: the templates under “Add symbol templates” in the menu add them. Existing triggers stay unchanged.",
  "manualC05S1B4": "Replacements build on each other: they run one after another in list order, and text one replacement inserts can be replaced by a later one. Example: “mfg” → “mit freundlichen Grüßen”, then “Grüßen” → “Grüßen 👋”. For overlapping exact triggers within one step, the longest trigger wins across all entries; ties use list order. Special characters in triggers such as C++, .NET and @handle are matched literally. Exact entries run first, then Similar ones. In a Similar entry, a trigger under 4 characters (or without a letter or digit) matches exactly; its longer triggers stay similar.",
  "manualC05S1B5": "Each replacement shows where it came from: “Manual” you added yourself, “Learned” came from your corrections, “Imported” from a file. The star marks a replacement as a favourite; starred ones come first in every sort order. When you save, the dialog checks the same rules as the rest of the dictionary: at most 60 characters per trigger, no trigger twice, and a trigger must not be exactly the new text.",
  "manualC05S1B6": "Change the order with “Change order” in the ⋯ menu or in the hint above the list: drag a row by its handle or move it up and down with the arrow buttons; every change is saved at once. If you don’t want a chain, turn off “Build replacements on each other” in the ⋯ menu. Each rule then only checks the dictated text and inserted text is never replaced again; where rules overlap, the one higher in the list wins. The test dialog uses the same setting.",
  "manualC05S1Heading": "How replacements work",
  "manualC05S2B1I1": "Exact: only the identical spelling is replaced.",
  "manualC05S2B1I2": "Similar: slightly different spellings are replaced too. This applies only to trigger phrases of at least 4 characters. New entries start as “Similar” with tolerance “Strict” when every trigger has at least 4 characters, otherwise as “Exact”. Another form of the same word, such as “projects” for the trigger “project”, is not replaced. Only a typical ending such as -s, -es or -ing counts as another form. When such an ending is missing or extra, WhisPaste does not replace the word, because that cannot be told apart from another form of it. Other differences at the end of a word are replaced.",
  "manualC05S2B1I3": "Tolerance (strict, standard, or tolerant): set per entry, not for all entries at once.",
  "manualC05S2B2": "Trigger phrases without letters or digits, such as ... or :), need at least 2 characters. They are replaced exactly as written, even inside a word. A single character such as ? is never replaced.",
  "manualC05S2Heading": "Matching",
  "manualC05S3B1": "You can back up or transfer your replacements as a CSV file. The file has the columns {k1}. Separate several trigger phrases with {k2}. A file can be at most {k3} and {k4} lines.",
  "@manualC05S3B1": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k2": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k3": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k4": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC05S3B2": "Import and export are in the More menu (⋯) above the list. The try-it field opens from the same menu.",
  "manualC05S3Heading": "Import and export (CSV)",
  "manualC05S4B1": "“Terms” holds names, jargon and abbreviations that speech recognition often gets wrong. They are passed to the recognizer as a hint. Only add what is really misrecognized: the list has a short limit, and words the recognizer already spells correctly just take up room.\n\nTerms imported from suggestions show “Learned”. After “As term”, Undo removes the added term and reopens the suggestion. Import terms in Dictionary → Terms or on the personal page of setup: choose or drop a CSV, text or JSON vocabulary file, check new/existing/invalid entries, select terms and confirm. For wrong/right pairs, only the right spelling becomes a term, not a replacement. Existing terms are kept; duplicates are merged. Limits: one file, 1 MiB, 5000 entries, 60 characters per term. Export CSV saves the term list locally.",
  "@manualC05S4B1": {"description": "Manual: what the terms list is for."},
  "manualC05S4B2": "If the list is empty and you said during setup what you use WhisPaste for, the tab suggests matching terms once, for example for development or law. All of them are selected. Deselect the ones you don’t need and click “Add”. “Hide” adds nothing. The suggestion does not come back after that.",
  "@manualC05S4B2": {"description": "Manual: the one-time role terms suggestion."},
  "manualC05S4B3": "Each term is one row. Type in the bar on top to filter the list, and press Enter to add what you typed as a new term; Cmd+F (macOS) or Ctrl+F (Windows, Linux) jumps to the bar. If the search finds nothing, the button under the message adds what you typed as a term. The pencil changes a term, the bin deletes it at once, and “Undo” in the message brings it back. A term that is already there, in any letter case, is not added twice. If the active speech engine does not accept terms, the tab says so at the top; “Add as a replacement” then leads to a new replacement.",
  "@manualC05S4B3": {"description": "Manual: the terms list with its bar, row actions and undo."},
  "manualC05S4B4": "When you correct a word in a recording and save, WhisPaste offers “Add to dictionary” right below the entry. One click adds the corrected word to the terms; a word that is already there is not offered. Everything stays on your computer.",
  "@manualC05S4B4": {"description": "Manual: offer to add a corrected word to the dictionary after editing a recording."},
  "manualC05S4B5": "The order of the terms is their priority: the recognition uses the list from the top, and when there is not room for all of them, the ones at the bottom are left out. The up arrow moves an important term higher. Below the line “From here on the recognition no longer uses the terms” the terms are paler: they stay saved, but the active recognition does not use them right now.",
  "@manualC05S4B5": {"description": "Manual: term order is priority; the line from which the recognition no longer uses the terms."},
  "manualC05S4Heading": "Terms",
  "@manualC05S4Heading": {"description": "Manual, Dictionary chapter: section about the terms list."},
  "manualC05S5B1": "Make the same correction twice and WhisPaste offers it in the “Suggestions” tab. A correction is the voice command “correct: …” while recording a note, a change you save to a recording’s text under “Recordings”, or a correction you make to the pasted text right in the target app (terminal, editor, browser). Rewording a whole passage, or changing only case or punctuation, does not count. Below the suggestions, the “Learning log” lists every correction WhisPaste noticed, with how often, from where and why nothing is suggested yet, and shows whether WhisPaste could follow your changes in the last target apps or why not; single entries can be removed.",
  "@manualC05S5B1": {"description": "Manual: what counts as a correction and when a suggestion appears."},
  "manualC05S5B2": "For each suggestion you choose: “As replacement” creates a replacement, “As term” puts the word under “Terms”, “Reject” drops it, and it will not come back while it stays in the learning log. The choice applies to that suggestion; you can delete terms and replacements later. Nothing is added without your confirmation. The “Learn from corrections” switch in the same tab turns learning off; existing entries stay as they are.",
  "@manualC05S5B2": {"description": "Manual: the choices for a suggestion and the learning switch."},
  "manualC05S5B3": "A correction made right in the target app counts too, for example in a terminal, an editor or a browser: for up to one minute after a paste, WhisPaste reads back only the pasted section, never the whole document and never a password field. The text stays on your device. This works only where the app shares its text through accessibility; the Mac App Store version cannot do it. The “Learn from corrections” switch turns this off as well. Everywhere, the Mac App Store version included, the “Learn correction” hotkey works (switch it on under “Keyboard shortcuts”): select the corrected word in the other app and press it, and the suggestion appears right away in the “Suggestions” tab.",
  "@manualC05S5B3": {"description": "Manual: learning from edits made right in the target app (#384)."},
  "manualC05S5Heading": "Suggestions from corrections",
  "@manualC05S5Heading": {"description": "Manual, Dictionary chapter: section about suggestions learned from corrections."},
  "manualC05Title": "Dictionary",
  "manualC06S1B1": "A snippet is a block of text you insert with a trigger word or a hotkey, such as a signature or a standard reply.",
  "manualC06S1B2I1": "“Static”: the text is inserted exactly as you saved it.",
  "manualC06S1B2I2": "“Interactive”: WhisPaste asks for individual fields one after another by recording, and places them in your template.",
  "manualC06S1B3": "Snippet or replacement? A replacement in the dictionary fixes a single word automatically in every dictation. A snippet is a whole text you call up on purpose. While the list is empty, example chips (signature, standard reply, interactive snippet) show what a snippet looks like: a click opens the editor beside the list pre-filled, and the example is kept as your snippet like typed text.",
  "manualC06S1Heading": "What snippets are",
  "manualC06S2B1": "There are two ways to open the picker:",
  "manualC06S2B2I1": "With the picker trigger word: start a recording and say only this word to open the picker. WhisPaste never listens permanently; the word works only during a recording. If you say it followed by the name of a snippet (for example “signature”), WhisPaste inserts the matching snippet directly. If the name is ambiguous, the picker opens with the name as its search term. This works only when the whole recording has this form; in the middle of a sentence the word has no effect. You set it in Settings under Keyboard shortcuts, group Notes & snippets, right below the picker hotkey. Leave it empty to turn the picker off.",
  "manualC06S2B2I2": "With the picker hotkey: off by default; turn it on under “Picker hotkey”.",
  "manualC06S2B3": "You can also dictate an interactive snippet in one sentence: in the picker, press {shortcut} or click the speech-bubble icon of the row. A preview then shows which value went into which field. You can change any value; Enter inserts, Esc cancels. With a text model set up, a natural sentence is enough; without one, say the field names along.",
  "@manualC06S2B3": {"placeholders": {"shortcut": {"type": "String", "example": "Cmd+Enter"}}},
  "manualC06S2B4": "Snippets are pasted as plain text: markers such as ** or # stay out of mail and chat. If an app should receive the Markdown, turn on “Paste snippets with Markdown” in the settings. The copy button of a recording or a note also copies plain text; “Copy as Markdown” in the “⋮” menu keeps the markers. Dictation is never changed.",
  "manualC06S2B5": "The picker stays open while you choose, even if a notification or another window briefly takes focus. It closes with Esc, when you pick a snippet, or when you click outside it. If another window has taken focus, the picker hotkey brings it back to the front and keeps your search. On Linux with Wayland it closes as soon as another window gets focus, because WhisPaste cannot see clicks in other windows there.",
  "manualC06S2Heading": "Opening the snippet picker",
  "manualC06S3B1I1": "Create a snippet with “Add” and choose “Static” or “Interactive”.",
  "manualC06S3B1I2": "For “Interactive”, define the fields and write the template. Put placeholders in double curly braces, such as {k1}.",
  "@manualC06S3B1I2": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC06S3B1I3": "Variables fill themselves when inserting: {vars}. Date and time take a format after the colon, such as {example}. Unknown variables stay in the text and are flagged while you edit.",
  "@manualC06S3B1I3": {
    "placeholders": {
      "vars": {"type": "String"},
      "example": {"type": "String"}
    }
  },
  "manualC06S3Heading": "Creating a snippet",
  "manualC06S4B1": "Use the sort button above the list to order snippets by title, date added or date changed, optionally in reverse. The default is A–Z by title. In the snippet picker, without a search term, snippets are also listed A–Z by title; with a search term, the best match comes first.",
  "manualC06S4Heading": "Sorting",
  "manualC06S5B1": "Clicking a snippet opens it beside the list, no dialog needed, and you write straight into title and text. It is saved as you type; Ctrl+S (Cmd+S on Mac) saves right away. Only a complete snippet is saved: if the title or the text is empty, a note above the text says why, and the picker keeps inserting the last saved version (“Restore saved version” brings it back). Such an incomplete state is kept while WhisPaste is running: the row shows “Draft”. The buttons at the top copy, star or delete it, “More” duplicates it; Esc or the X closes it. The star marks a snippet as a favourite; favourites are listed first. “Delete” moves a snippet to the trash (“Trash” under “Filter” next to the search), where you can bring it back with “Restore” or remove it with “Delete for good”. Snippets in the trash do not appear in the picker and are removed automatically after the period you set under “Auto-delete trash after” in Privacy & data (never, if set to “Never”).",
  "manualC06S5Heading": "Favourites and trash",
  "manualC06S6B1": "Press Space on the highlighted row, hold Ctrl (Cmd on macOS) while clicking a row, or click the select button next to the sort button above the list. A bar appears with the number of marked snippets and “Select all”. From there you can move all marked snippets to the trash (an “Undo” message names the number), add or remove the star, or export them as TXT, Markdown or JSON. Esc or “Cancel” leaves the selection.",
  "manualC06S6Heading": "Selecting several snippets",
  "manualC06S7B1": "In the note editor, the transcript editor and the snippet editor, press {shortcut} or click the insert button in the toolbar. Search, pick with the arrow keys and Enter, and the snippet lands at the cursor or replaces the selected text. In a note, the bookmark button saves the selected text as a new snippet; the snippet then shows which note it came from.",
  "@manualC06S7B1": {
    "description": "Manual: inserting snippets from the in-app editors (#664).",
    "placeholders": {
      "shortcut": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "Ctrl+Shift+I"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC06S7Heading": "Inserting from the editors",
  "manualC06Title": "Snippets",
  "manualC07S1B1": "The Notes area collects text, such as ideas or research. Recordings go straight into a note when you use the quick note. When you search, the list becomes a list of hits: notes whose title matches come first, then tags, then text.",
  "manualC07S1B2I1": "“Add to favorites” highlights a note. Favorites are listed first under their own heading; click the heading to fold or unfold them.",
  "manualC07S1B2I2": "“Add tag” assigns a note to a category. The field suggests the tags you already use: choose one with the arrow keys and Enter.",
  "manualC07S1B2I3": "Without a mouse: the down arrow in the search field moves into the list, Enter opens the note with the cursor in its text, Esc goes back to the list and a second Esc closes the note. Esc in the search field clears it; Ctrl/Cmd+N creates a new note.",
  "manualC07S1B2I4": "The head is laid out as on Recordings and takes one row: the search, the “Filter” button, the sort menu, “Group by date” and “New note”. Under “Filter” you choose Notes or Trash and your tags. Active filters show as small marks next to the search; their X removes them.",
  "manualC07S1B2I5": "The book button in the head switches to the reading view: headings, bold, lists, quotes and code appear formatted, checklists (- [ ]) as boxes. A click or the space bar ticks an item, which then reads - [x] in the text. “Check all”, “Reset” and “Move done to bottom” act on every checklist of the note. Recordings show their text the same way and can be ticked there too.",
  "manualC07S1B2I6": "Formatting without a mouse: Cmd+B or Ctrl+B for bold, Cmd+I or Ctrl+I for italic. With Cmd+Shift (macOS) or Ctrl+Shift (Windows, Linux): H heading, L bulleted list, 7 numbered list, T checklist, 9 quote, C code. Pressing it again removes the format. Tab moves from the text into the toolbar; there the arrow keys switch buttons, Home and End jump to the first and last, and Enter applies the format. The shortcuts also work in Recordings and in snippets.",
  "manualC07S1B2I7": "Several notes at once: the select button in the head, Ctrl/Cmd+click or Space ticks notes, Shift+click or Shift+arrow ticks a range, Ctrl/Cmd+A ticks all. The bar above the list puts the ticked notes in the trash after one confirmation, and its menu adds a tag, marks them as favorites, exports or merges them (Ctrl+M). One “Undo” takes the whole action back. In the trash the bar restores them. Esc ends the selection.",
  "manualC07S1Heading": "Working with notes",
  "manualC07S2B1": "“Move to trash” puts a note in the trash. It stays there until you delete it for good. Notes are removed automatically after the period set under “Auto-delete trash after” in Privacy & data (not at all if set to “Never”).",
  "manualC07S2B2I1": "“Delete forever” removes the note from the trash.",
  "manualC07S2Heading": "Trash",
  "manualC07S3B1": "The Notes area shows which note is the quick note. Details are in the quick note chapter.",
  "manualC07S3Heading": "Quick note",
  "manualC07S4B1": "When you add an image, WhisPaste removes metadata such as the location (GPS), camera data, and EXIF, XMP and IPTC information. Only the cleaned copy is stored.",
  "manualC07S4Heading": "Images and privacy",
  "manualC07S5B1": "Long notes stay smooth, even with more than 100,000 words: WhisPaste only builds the part you can see. Selection, search, dictation at the cursor and screen readers work across the whole text. Use “Go to paragraph” in the editor menu to jump to a paragraph number. There is no length limit; from about one million characters a notice suggests “Split note”, which divides the text at the cursor into two notes. Search waits a moment after your last keystroke, and in long notes autosave saves at most every few seconds.",
  "manualC07S5Heading": "Very long notes",
  "manualC07S6B1": "Focus mode hides the sidebar, the title bar and the note list, so only the open note fills the screen. Switch it on with the focus button in the editor toolbar, “Focus mode” in the editor menu or {k1}; the same shortcut or Esc ends it. The dock stays, so you can keep dictating. “Paragraph focus” in the bar above the note dims every paragraph except the one you are writing; it is off at first and WhisPaste remembers your choice. Dimming switches without animation.",
  "@manualC07S6B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC07S6Heading": "Focus mode",
  "manualC07S7B1": "The arrow buttons at the start of the toolbar undo and redo changes in notes, in a recording's transcript and in snippets. Shortcuts: Cmd+Z and Cmd+Shift+Z (macOS), Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z (Windows, Linux).",
  "manualC07S7B2": "Keys typed in quick succession undo together; a pause or a cursor jump starts a new step. Formatting, “Replace all matches”, dictation and smart actions are steps of their own. The history survives autosave, and when you come back to a note you have not changed elsewhere, you can still undo what you did before.",
  "manualC07S7Heading": "Undo and redo",
  "manualC07Title": "Notes",
  "manualC08S1B1": "Recordings shows every entry: the recognized text, with its date. If audio was saved, the audio file belongs to that entry. When WhisPaste knows which app the text went to, the entry shows it with its name and icon. WhisPaste asks the system for both and keeps them only on this device; they are deleted once no entry names that app any more.",
  "manualC08S1Heading": "What recordings hold",
  "manualC08S2B1I1": "Type {k1} to filter by tags.",
  "@manualC08S2B1I1": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC08S2B1I2": "Type {k1} and a language to filter by language.",
  "@manualC08S2B1I2": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC08S2B1I3": "Or just type a keyword.",
  "manualC08S2B1I4": "The “Filter” button next to the search opens the filters: “All”, “Favorites”, “Archived” and “Trash”, with “Period” and “Duration” below. Each active filter shows as a small mark next to the search; its X removes it, and the number on the button counts them.",
  "manualC08S2B1I5": "Under “Period” you narrow the list to today, yesterday, the last 7 or 30 days or this month. Your choice writes {k1} into the search field for you.",
  "@manualC08S2B1I5": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC08S2B1I6": "Chips below the period chips limit the length of a recording: “Under 1 min”, “1–5 min” and “Over 5 min”. For finer limits type {k1} or {k2} yourself.",
  "@manualC08S2B1I6": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k2": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC08S2B2": "If search misses recordings you know exist, or previews are missing, choose “{k1}” in {k2}. Search and previews are rebuilt from your recordings, newest first; your texts are not changed. If WhisPaste finds a damaged search index at start, it rebuilds it by itself and tells you.",
  "@manualC08S2B2": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k2": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC08S2Heading": "Search and filter",
  "manualC08S3B1I1": "“Copy text” copies the text to the clipboard again.",
  "manualC08S3B1I2": "“Add to favorites” keeps an entry at the top. “Remove from favorites” undoes that.",
  "manualC08S3B1I3": "“Archive” moves an entry out of the main list without deleting it.",
  "manualC08S3B1I4": "“Duplicate”, “Merge” (several entries into one), and “Select multiple” help you tidy up.",
  "manualC08S3B1I5": "“Add annotation” and “Add tag…” add details to an entry.",
  "manualC08S3B1I6": "“Transcribe again” runs the saved recording through speech recognition once more and through the same text steps as a dictation (filler words, written forms, dictionary). It replaces the text only after you confirm; the previous text stays as the original. If a transcription failed with an error that can be repeated, “Try again” on the entry starts it again with the same recording, without asking. Both only work while the recording is saved.",
  "manualC08S3B2": "When you edit the text of an entry, WhisPaste keeps earlier states of it. The clock icon next to “Edit” opens the versions: each shows when it was kept, how it came about (dictated, typed, converted, restored) and its length. “Restore” puts that text back; the text it replaces stays in the list, so you can undo the restore. Typing within ten minutes is one version, and at most 50 versions are kept per entry, oldest first. The original text and the Smart Mode result are not part of this list and stay as they are. “Compare with current text” shows word by word what changed since that version (added words underlined, removed ones struck through, each also labelled), and “Take over this section” brings back a single section instead of the whole text; the replaced text stays in the list here too.",
  "manualC08S3B3": "Recordings, notes and snippets share one editor: the same bar above the text with undo, redo, formatting and find and replace, the same shortcuts, and below the text the word count with reading time. Your text is saved as you type; Ctrl+S (Cmd+S on the Mac) saves right away. A recording's transcript is written directly in the surface too, with no edit, save or cancel button; the original text stays as a version, and one editing session makes exactly one version.",
  "manualC08S3Heading": "Managing entries",
  "manualC08S4B1": "Start a voice note on the entry. Say “tag: name” to set a tag, or “correct: text” to replace the entry’s text.",
  "manualC08S4B2": "A correction replaces the entry’s whole text, not just one word. “korrektur: text” works the same way. Right afterwards you can undo the change from the notice.",
  "manualC08S4Heading": "Voice note",
  "manualC08S5B1": "With “PIN protection”, a PIN is required before recordings are shown. This also applies to the side panel, the menu of the floating button, and the automation API. The PIN keeps others from casually viewing your transcriptions, but it does not encrypt them. Notes are locked the same way. With a PIN set, the tray menu lists recent recordings by time and word count instead of their first words.",
  "manualC08S5B2": "A forgotten PIN cannot be recovered. To reset it, all transcriptions and saved audio files (including archive and trash), all notes with their versions, and all note images are deleted immediately. Settings, snippets, and replacements are kept.",
  "manualC08S5B3": "While the recordings are locked by a PIN, the clipboard tab in the side panel shows no copied text and pastes none. Copied text comes back once you unlock them.",
  "manualC08S5Heading": "PIN protection",
  "manualC08S6B1": "Under “Auto-delete trash after”, set how long deleted entries stay in the trash before they are removed automatically.",
  "manualC08S6Heading": "Automatic emptying",
  "manualC08S7B1": "You can turn saving off in {k1}. A dictation is then only pasted or copied to the clipboard. It does not appear in Recordings, and no audio is kept. Existing recordings stay until you delete them. Statistics and the rating reminder keep working. Imported files still go to Recordings, because you pick them for exactly that.",
  "manualC08S7Heading": "Turning recording storage off",
  "manualC08Title": "Recordings",
  "manualC09S1B1": "You can have existing audio files transcribed. Choose “{k1}” and pick the file. The first audio track of MP4, MOV, MKV and WebM videos is extracted locally. The video is never uploaded. Use the file picker or drag files into WhisPaste.",
  "@manualC09S1B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC09S1B2": "Besides {k1}, you can import more formats depending on your system: on Mac {k2}, on Linux {k3}, on Windows {k4}.",
  "@manualC09S1B2": {
    "placeholders": {
      "k1": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k2": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k3": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "k4": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "manualC09S1B3": "On Linux, M4A/AAC needs the GStreamer AAC decoder. The .deb package installs it as a recommended package. If it is missing in a .deb or tarball install, install it on Debian or Ubuntu with this command and restart WhisPaste. The Flatpak build has not been verified to contain the decoder, so M4A/AAC may be missing there, and this command does not apply. The Snap build should contain the decoder, but this is not verified yet. If it is missing there, convert the file to WAV.",
  "manualC09S1B4": "You can also start in your file manager. If you installed WhisPaste on Windows with the installer from the website, right-click an audio file ({k1}) and choose “Transcribe with WhisPaste” (on Windows 11 under “Show more options”). In the Microsoft Store version, on a Mac and on Linux, choose “Open With” and then WhisPaste. If WhisPaste is already open, it takes the file over. WhisPaste never becomes the default app for audio files.",
  "@manualC09S1B4": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC09S1Cmd": "sudo apt install gstreamer1.0-libav",
  "manualC09S1Heading": "Transcribing files",
  "manualC09S2B2": "What to do: split the recording into shorter parts and import them one by one.",
  "manualC09S2Heading": "Limits",
  "manualC09S3B2": "What to do: try again. If that does not help, restart WhisPaste or choose another model under {k1}.",
  "@manualC09S3B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC09S3Heading": "Import errors",
  "manualC09Title": "Importing audio and video files",
  "manualC10S1B1": "The statistics have three areas: {k1}, {k2}, and {k3}.",
  "@manualC10S1B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}, "k3": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC10S1B2": "Use {k1} and {k2} to choose the time range.",
  "@manualC10S1B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC10S1Heading": "The areas",
  "manualC10S2B1I1": "{k1} and {k2} count your recordings and how long you recorded. Words per minute and days dictated come from the same counts; no text or audio is ever used for them.",
  "@manualC10S2B1I1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC10S2B1I2": "{k1} is a rough estimate: WhisPaste counts two thirds of the recording time as saved, because speaking is usually faster than typing. It is not a measurement.",
  "@manualC10S2B1I2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC10S2B1I3": "{k1}: local recordings save cloud costs. WhisPaste estimates this at {k2} per minute, a fixed reference rate.",
  "@manualC10S2B1I3": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC10S2B1I4": "The activity panel shows the last 16 weeks day by day. A streak forgives one missed day. All figures come from the counts kept for the statistics, so deleting recordings or turning off saving does not change them; only Reset clears them.",
  "manualC10S2Heading": "What the numbers mean",
  "manualC10S3B1": "“{k1}” deletes all statistics data. WhisPaste asks first, and this cannot be undone.",
  "@manualC10S3B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC10S3Heading": "Reset",
  "manualC10Title": "Statistics",
  "manualC11S1B1": "Settings are grouped into sections. The most important ones:",
  "manualC11S1B2I1": "{k1}: how the app looks.",
  "@manualC11S1B2I1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S1B2I10": "{k1}: more options, such as reset.",
  "@manualC11S1B2I10": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S1B2I11": "{k1}: version, update check, links, and licences.",
  "@manualC11S1B2I11": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S1B2I2": "{k1}: model and recognition language.",
  "@manualC11S1B2I2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S1B2I3": "{k1}: microphone and volume. By default WhisPaste evens out quiet recordings automatically after you speak; with that off, you set the input volume by hand.",
  "@manualC11S1B2I3": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S1B2I4": "{k1}: preset, target language, and provider.",
  "@manualC11S1B2I4": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S1B2I5": "{k1}: what happens after a recording.",
  "@manualC11S1B2I5": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S1B2I6": "{k1}: hotkeys.",
  "@manualC11S1B2I6": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S1B2I7": "{k1}: trash period.",
  "@manualC11S1B2I7": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S1B2I8": "{k1}: export, import, and automatic backup.",
  "@manualC11S1B2I8": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S1B2I9": "{k1}: local API and event hooks for programs on this computer, including appending to, replacing text in, tagging and pinning notes by note ID.",
  "@manualC11S1B2I9": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S1Heading": "The sections",
  "manualC11S2B1I1": "{k1} saves your settings automatically after changes. The last five files are kept. It is off by default.",
  "@manualC11S2B1I1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S2B1I2": "{k1} immediately deletes all transcriptions, audio files and settings and resets WhisPaste. Read the confirmation carefully before you confirm.",
  "@manualC11S2B1I2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC11S2Heading": "Back up and reset",
  "manualC11Title": "Settings",
  "manualC12S1B1": "By default, speech recognition runs on your device. Audio goes to a cloud service only when you actively choose a cloud provider. Transcriptions are stored in a local database on your device, and saved audio files as files next to it. Audio is kept only while “{k1}” is on, for {days} days at most. Directly below it, choose 5, 10, 20, 50 or 100 recordings; the default is {count}. A lower count takes effect at the next cleanup, not immediately when selected. Failed recordings kept for a retry do not count towards this limit.",
  "@manualC12S1B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "count": {"type": "int"}, "days": {"type": "int"}}},
  "manualC12S1B2": "The update check asks GitHub for new versions. Feedback is sent only when you submit it.",
  "manualC12S1B3": "If a transcription fails because the server or the connection is not available, the audio of that recording is kept for up to {days} days, so you can try again from the error message or under “Privacy & data”. This also applies when audio retention is off. If it is on, the normal retention applies instead.",
  "@manualC12S1B3": {"placeholders": {"days": {"type": "int"}}},
  "manualC12S1B3Ref": "If a transcription fails because the server or the connection is not available, the audio of that recording is kept for the period you set under “Privacy & data”, so you can try again from the error message or there. This also applies when audio retention is off. If it is on, the normal retention applies instead. You can still choose the period at any time; it applies once retention is off.",
  "manualC12S1B4": "Recordings that were interrupted, for example by a crash or by quitting the app, are processed once at the next start and their audio is deleted afterwards. This also applies when audio retention is off.",
  "manualC12S1B5Ref": "How many recordings stay saved and when older ones move to the trash is set under “Privacy & data”.",
  "manualC12S1Heading": "What stays on your device",
  "manualC12S2B1": "The providers OpenAI and Deepgram need your own API key. Enter it under {k1}.",
  "@manualC12S2B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC12S2B2": "Custom endpoint: for a server on your network or this device (LM Studio, Ollama, llama.cpp) enter its address ending in /v1, an optional key and the model name. Check reachability, key and model with “Test connection”.",
  "manualC12S2B3": "Where audio and text go: on this device (localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1) everything stays here. In the local network (private addresses such as 192.168.x.x and .local names) it stays in your network; other devices there can read unencrypted http. A server on the internet receives the audio (transcription) or the text (Smart Mode) and is reachable only over https.",
  "manualC12S2B4": "The transcription path is fixed at /audio/transcriptions, as in OpenAI; Smart Mode uses /chat/completions. The server must offer both under the address ending in /v1.",
  "manualC12S2Heading": "Cloud providers",
  "manualC12S3B1": "Two options are on by default: {k1} and {k2}. You can turn both off in {k3}.",
  "@manualC12S3B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}, "k3": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC12S3Heading": "Usage statistics and error reports",
  "manualC12S4B1": "WhisPaste protects your recordings on this device with its own random key. The key is created on this device, stays in your system’s key store (macOS keychain, encrypted with your Windows account on Windows, the Secret Service on Linux) and never leaves the device, not even in a settings export.",
  "manualC12S4B2": "Your system only asks when you tap {k1}: during setup on the privacy page, or once in a short note if you set up WhisPaste before. Nothing asks at app start. The status and the way to allow it later are under {k2} → {k3}.",
  "@manualC12S4B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}, "k3": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC12S4B3": "If you decline or cancel, WhisPaste keeps working, just without this extra protection, and an existing key is never replaced. On Linux without a Secret Service, WhisPaste keeps the key in a file only your user can read and says so in the app.",
  "manualC12S4B4": "Once the key is created, back it up in Settings → Privacy with “Back up”: as a recovery code to write down or copy, or as a backup file. Both hold the same key and need no password; a checksum in the code catches typos. The code and the file are as valuable as the key itself: keep them somewhere safe, not next to your data and not unprotected in a cloud. Afterwards the row shows when you last backed up.",
  "manualC12S4B5": "Under {k1} → {k2} the key shows the one step its state needs: {k3} while it is in place, with a note whether it was backed up already; {k4} when it is missing; {k5} when your system’s key store does not answer. There is no way to delete only the key, because your recordings could no longer be read. It is removed only together with that data, through {k6}.",
  "@manualC12S4B5": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}, "k3": {"type": "String"}, "k4": {"type": "String"}, "k5": {"type": "String"}, "k6": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC12S4B6": "If the key is lost, Settings → Privacy offers “{k1}”: enter the recovery code, or choose or drop the backup file. A wrong or damaged backup gives a clear message and changes nothing; a backup of a different key is refused. The same works on a new device with your copied data folder. If you have no backup left, “{k2}” starts over after a warning: data encrypted with the old key stays unreadable, and the old files are kept under a new name instead of being overwritten.",
  "@manualC12S4B6": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC12S4B7": "No way back to 1.4: from version 1.5 on, WhisPaste stores your recordings, notes, dictionary and snippets encrypted with this key. Version 1.4.x cannot read them, so do not reinstall 1.4.x after the update. If a 1.5 version causes trouble, install the newest 1.5.x update instead and tell us about the problem; your data stays readable there.",
  "manualC12S4Heading": "Data key",
  "manualC12S5B1": "Under {k1} → {k2} you see the permissions your system asks for. On the Mac these are {k3} for recording, {k4} for pasting automatically (Accessibility) and {k5} for a single key as the trigger; Windows asks only for {k3}, Linux for none, so the section is not there. Each row shows its status and the one step that changes it.",
  "@manualC12S5B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}, "k3": {"type": "String"}, "k4": {"type": "String"}, "k5": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC12S5B2": "WhisPaste needs {k1} only when a single key starts your recording, held or tapped twice; a key combination works without it. On the Mac it is a permission of its own, Windows has no such permission, and on Linux the single key needs an X11 session. If you turn it off on the Mac later, WhisPaste notices after a short while: your key combination takes over, a notice explains why and the row shows \"{k2}\". {k3} in the notice or in the row turns it back on; as soon as it is granted, the single key starts a dictation again, without a restart.",
  "@manualC12S5B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}, "k3": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC12S6B1": "The “News” block shows the newest entries of RSS or Atom feeds you choose, such as the WhisPaste blog. It is off by default. Switch it on under “Customise overview” and pick up to five feeds under “Sources”. Clicking an entry opens it in your browser.",
  "manualC12S6B2": "WhisPaste asks each feed at most once an hour, only while the overview is open and the window is active, never in the background or at start-up. Only the title, source and date are shown, no article text. As with any page you open in a browser, the feed's provider sees your IP address. Without a connection, the entries loaded last stay in place.",
  "manualC12S6Heading": "News on the overview",
  "manualC12S7B1": "The “{k1}” card is off by default. Switch it on under “{k2}” and pick a place by searching. WhisPaste does not use location services. It stores only the name and the coordinates rounded to two decimal places.",
  "@manualC12S7B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC12S7B2": "The search goes to OpenStreetMap (Nominatim) only when you press “{k1}”. WhisPaste asks MET Norway for the weather directly, only while the overview is open and at most once an hour. Both services see your IP address. Offline, the card shows the weather it loaded last.",
  "@manualC12S7B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC12S7Heading": "Weather on the overview",
  "manualC12Title": "Privacy and offline use",
  "manualC13S1B1": "WhisPaste recommends at least {k1} of memory. With less, the app still starts and recommends the “Fast” speech model.",
  "@manualC13S1B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC13S1Heading": "Memory",
  "manualC13S2B1": "WhisPaste uses the graphics card when it can speed up speech recognition:",
  "manualC13S2B2I1": "{k1}: {k2}, Apple’s graphics interface.",
  "@manualC13S2B2I1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC13S2B2I2": "{k1} and {k2} with AMD or Intel graphics: {k3}, a widely used graphics interface.",
  "@manualC13S2B2I2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}, "k3": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC13S2B2I3": "NVIDIA cards also use {k1}. With the optional CUDA pack, recognition also uses {k2}.",
  "@manualC13S2B2I3": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC13S2B2I4": "Without a suitable graphics card, WhisPaste computes on the processor ({k1}). That is slower.",
  "@manualC13S2B2I4": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC13S2Heading": "Graphics card",
  "manualC13S3B1": "The CUDA pack is an optional add-on for NVIDIA cards, per the app for {k1} and {k2}. It is available for {k3} and {k4} (x64). Turn it on under {k5}.",
  "@manualC13S3B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}, "k3": {"type": "String"}, "k4": {"type": "String"}, "k5": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC13S3B2": "The download is one-time. The app shows its size.",
  "manualC13S3B3": "The pack contains NVIDIA components. Before the first download you accept the NVIDIA CUDA licence (version 2026-01-26); the consent applies to this installation. Without a valid consent the row shows “Consent required”, and WhisPaste uses Vulkan or the CPU until you accept. If the licence text or the pack changes, you are asked again.",
  "manualC13S3B4": "After the download, WhisPaste briefly compares the pack with Vulkan once: the same speech model, a short reference sentence, in the background and never during a dictation. The faster one stays active. If Vulkan is faster, as it usually is on GTX 16 cards, the pack stays installed but unused, and the row offers to remove it. After a driver update, a new app version or with another speech model, WhisPaste measures again.",
  "manualC13S3Heading": "The CUDA pack",
  "manualC13S4B1": "Under {k1}, choose whether WhisPaste uses the graphics card. Processor only is usually slower.",
  "@manualC13S4B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC13S4Heading": "Graphics acceleration",
  "manualC13S5B1": "The Whisper models come in three tiers: Fast, Balanced, and Accurate. More accurate tiers usually recognise better but need more processing time.",
  "manualC13S5Heading": "Models and size",
  "manualC13S6B1": "On the overview, “Last dictation” shows whether the last dictation was processed on the processor or the graphics card, and how long the recognition took. If the graphics card was intended but did not take the work, it says “Fallback: the graphics card was intended, but the processor handled the dictation.”",
  "manualC13S6B2": "“WhisPaste storage” shows the space used by speech models, recordings and the cache. “Clean up…” empties only the cache, and only after asking. Speech models and recordings stay untouched.",
  "manualC13S6Heading": "Overview: last dictation and storage",
  "manualC13S7B1": "“System” shows at the top what you dictate with: speech recognition, speech model and microphone. Below are the load of the whole processor, the memory in use and the free space on WhisPaste's drive. The card is off by default. Switch it on under “Customise overview”.",
  "manualC13S7B2": "The values are measured only while the overview is open and the window is active. When memory runs low or the drive is nearly full, a note appears in the card.",
  "manualC13S7B3": "Speech recognition, speech model and microphone follow your settings at once. When something is missing, the card says so, for example “not downloaded”, “loads with the next dictation” or “no microphone found”. When the chosen microphone is not plugged in, the card says so after its name.",
  "manualC13S7Heading": "The “System” card on the overview",
  "manualC13S8B1": "If this computer has no connection to the download server, WhisPaste cannot fetch a speech model. Download the file on another computer and copy it into the model folder yourself.",
  "manualC13S8B2": "Step 1: Download one of these files. Use the copy button to get the address and paste it into a browser.",
  "manualC13S8B3": "Step 2: Copy the file into the model folder of your system, keeping its name unchanged. Create the folders if they do not exist.",
  "manualC13S8B4": "Step 3: Restart WhisPaste. It looks for the file by its name when it starts and then lists the speech model as installed.",
  "manualC13S8Heading": "Install speech model manually",
  "manualC13S8Linux": "Linux (the folder follows XDG_CONFIG_HOME if that is set):",
  "manualC13S8LinuxFlatpak": "Linux, Flatpak version:",
  "manualC13S8MacDirect": "macOS, version from the website:",
  "manualC13S8MacStore": "macOS, Mac App Store version:",
  "manualC13S8Model": "{k1}: file {k2}, {k3}.",
  "@manualC13S8Model": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}, "k3": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC13S8Parakeet": "Parakeet ({k2}) consists of four files: download all four. In step 2 they go into the subfolder {k1} of the model folder, with exactly this name, even if the download page has -int8 in its name.",
  "@manualC13S8Parakeet": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC13S8Tip": "WhisPaste recognises a Whisper model by its file name and the Parakeet files by name and size. So make sure every download is complete.",
  "manualC13S8WinDirect": "Windows, installer version:",
  "manualC13S8WinStore": "Windows, Microsoft Store version (replace the * with the rest of the folder name):",
  "manualC13Title": "Hardware and performance",
  "manualC14HangB2": "If WhisPaste stops responding for about 20 seconds (the dictation key and the tray icon no longer react), it records a short diagnosis and restarts itself, at most once every 10 minutes. A dictation that was running at that moment is kept up to that point and turned into text after the restart. If crash reports are on, one report goes to the developers, never with your audio or text. If it happens again within 10 minutes, WhisPaste does not restart again; quit and start it yourself.",
  "manualC14HangHeading": "WhisPaste stopped responding",
  "manualC14S10B2": "What to do: enter the key again under {k1}, or switch to on-device recognition.",
  "@manualC14S10B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S10Heading": "Cloud key missing or invalid",
  "manualC14S11B2": "What to do: nothing. You can choose the model again later in Settings.",
  "manualC14S11Heading": "Graphics memory full",
  "manualC14S12B2": "What to do: usually nothing. If it happens often, check the setting under {k1} and update the graphics driver.",
  "@manualC14S12B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S12Heading": "Calculated on the processor",
  "manualC14S13B2": "What to do: allow WhisPaste accessibility access in the system settings. If the message stays, restart WhisPaste.",
  "manualC14S13Heading": "Auto-Paste blocked (macOS)",
  "manualC14S13HotkeyB2": "The notice is a warning, not an error. It names an app that can disturb the hotkey. WhisPaste does not change anything in that app.",
  "manualC14S13HotkeyHeading": "Hotkey disturbed (secure keyboard input or another app, macOS)",
  "manualC14S13HotkeyStep1": "If the hotkey works reliably for you, you need to do nothing. Close the notice. It does not come back for this app.",
  "manualC14S13HotkeyStep2": "Keyboard protected (secure keyboard input): if a macOS password prompt is open, enter the password or close it. Otherwise leave the password field or quit the named app. The hotkey then works normally again.",
  "manualC14S13HotkeyStep3": "If the hotkey misses presses, quit the named app as a test. If that helps, switch off the app's keyboard feature, for example shortcuts, text replacement or macros.",
  "manualC14S13HotkeyStep4": "If it says “Siri”: open System Settings, then “Apple Intelligence & Siri” (“Siri & Spotlight” on older Macs), and set “Keyboard shortcut” to “Off”. Or switch Siri off there.",
  "manualC14S13HotkeyStep5": "If that does not help, try a key combination instead of a single key or a double tap under Settings → Keyboard shortcuts.",
  "manualC14S13PasswordB2": "What to do: paste the text by hand, it is on the clipboard and under “Recordings”. WhisPaste never pastes automatically into a password or PIN field. To tell, it only reads whether the field is a password field, never its content. If that is unclear, WhisPaste pastes as usual.",
  "manualC14S13PasswordHeading": "Auto-Paste skipped (password field, Windows)",
  "manualC14S13SecureB2": "While a password prompt or a password field is open, macOS protects the keyboard. WhisPaste then does not paste automatically; the text is on the clipboard. What to do: if macOS asks for a password (administrator, keychain, login), enter it or close the prompt. If the notice names an app, leave the password field there or quit the app. In Terminal and iTerm2, turn off “Secure Keyboard Entry” in the menu. Then paste the text with ⌘V.",
  "manualC14S13SecureHeading": "Not pasted automatically: keyboard protected (secure keyboard input, macOS)",
  "manualC14S14B2": "What to do: start WhisPaste as an administrator too.",
  "manualC14S14Heading": "Auto-Paste blocked (Windows)",
  "manualC14S15AppImageHeading": "AppImage",
  "@manualC14S15AppImageHeading": {
    "description": "Subheading for the AppImage build, a Linux app package format. Keep the name as is."
  },
  "manualC14S15B1": "On Linux, auto-paste uses a virtual input device (uinput). If it does not work, check whether WhisPaste may access that device.",
  "@manualC14S15B1": {
    "description": "Explains that auto-paste on Linux uses a virtual input device (uinput), and what to check when it does not work."
  },
  "manualC14S15B2": "The .deb package grants no access when it is installed. Auto-Paste asks first: in the setup or under Settings → After transcription, choose “Allow (administrator password)” and enter your administrator password. While the permission is in place, other programs in your session can create key presses too. “Withdraw permission” takes it back at any time; uninstalling removes it as well. If another rule on your system (for example from Steam) gives your session access to the virtual keyboard (uaccess), the access stays even after you withdraw the permission.",
  "@manualC14S15B2": {
    "description": "The .deb flow (R-02): nothing is granted on install; the user allows /dev/uinput in the app with the administrator password and can remove it again."
  },
  "manualC14S15B3": "The Flatpak build gives the app access to the system's input devices so that auto-paste can work. WhisPaste itself does not read input devices.",
  "@manualC14S15B3": {
    "description": "Flatpak note: what the sandbox permission gives the app. Plain language, no technical flags."
  },
  "manualC14S15B3E": "The rule gives write access only to the signed-in session. These commands need administrator rights (sudo) and change the system. If you do not want this, leave auto-paste off and paste the text by hand. To remove the rule, use the button in the settings that copies the matching command.",
  "@manualC14S15B3E": {
    "description": "Warning that the udev rule gives write access to the signed-in session only, that the commands need administrator rights, and how to remove the rule."
  },
  "manualC14S15B3S": "Auto-Paste also needs write access to /dev/uinput on the host. Install the udev rule 71-whispaste-uinput.rules into /etc/udev/rules.d/ and then run these commands:",
  "@manualC14S15B3S": {
    "description": "Lead-in before the udev command block (Flatpak). The commands follow in their own copyable block."
  },
  "manualC14S15B3W": "With this access, the app could in theory read keystrokes of other programs if your user can read the input devices (e.g. as a member of the input group). Members of the input group can also generate virtual input.",
  "@manualC14S15B3W": {
    "description": "Security warning about access to input devices in the Flatpak build."
  },
  "manualC14S15B4": "In the Snap build, run this once in a terminal:",
  "@manualC14S15B4": {
    "description": "Lead-in before the Snap command; the command itself follows in its own copyable block."
  },
  "manualC14S15B5": "The AppImage needs access to /dev/uinput. In the setup or in the settings, copy the command and run it once in a terminal. It installs the udev rule 71-whispaste-uinput.rules into /etc/udev/rules.d/, which gives write access only to the signed-in session. Until then, auto-paste only copies the text. Settings show the command to remove it again in the same place.",
  "@manualC14S15B5": {
    "description": "AppImage setup: the copied udev command (session access only) and what happens until setup is done."
  },
  "manualC14S15Cmd": "sudo udevadm control --reload-rules &&\nsudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=misc --sysname-match=uinput",
  "@manualC14S15Cmd": {
    "description": "Shell command shown in a copyable block. Keep the command unchanged."
  },
  "manualC14S15EnableExtCmd": "gnome-extensions enable whispaste-focus@whispaste.de",
  "@manualC14S15EnableExtCmd": {"description": "Terminal command, not translated."},
  "manualC14S15Fallback": "Until then, auto-paste only copies the text.",
  "@manualC14S15Fallback": {
    "description": "Sentence after the setup options: until setup is done, auto-paste only copies the text."
  },
  "manualC14S15FlatpakHeading": "Flatpak",
  "@manualC14S15FlatpakHeading": {
    "description": "Subheading for the Flatpak build, a Linux app package format. Keep the name as is."
  },
  "manualC14S15G1": "Hold or tap: Hold the key longer than the threshold (default 250 ms) and it works like push-to-talk. A short tap starts and stops a recording.",
  "manualC14S15G2": "Single key: instead of a key combination you can use a single key as the dictation hotkey, left and right apart. Holding it dictates until you let go. Two quick taps (within 400 ms) start hands-free dictation; one press ends it. The same works with a key combination; turn \"Double tap: dictate hands-free\" off under \"Keyboard shortcuts\". Choose the key under \"Keyboard shortcuts\" → \"Change hotkey\".",
  "manualC14S15G3": "Single key: Shift, Control, Alt or Option, and Command, Windows or Super are possible, each left or right, plus Fn (Mac only). Left keys and Shift start only after you hold them alone for a moment, so ordinary shortcuts trigger nothing. On Linux with Wayland single keys are not available. If the key cannot be used on the device, the saved key combination applies in its place; your choice is kept, and “Change” picks another trigger.",
  "manualC14S15G4": "Permission granted later: if WhisPaste cannot observe the single key, it tries again as soon as you bring WhisPaste back to the front. On the Mac, WhisPaste first asks for Input Monitoring. If you grant it later in System Settings, the key then works without a restart. This only applies while the key combination runs as a stand-in. On Windows, Windows may refuse to let WhisPaste observe the key. On Linux the key cannot be used when the session does not report key releases. On the Mac, Settings → Privacy & data → Permissions shows where Input Monitoring stands, and lets you grant it there.",
  "manualC14S15H1": "Under Wayland, an app gets system-wide shortcuts only through the desktop. KDE Plasma and GNOME 48 or newer provide them. Older GNOME versions, such as in Ubuntu 24.04, cannot: WhisPaste then offers “{k1}” under {k2} and adds the hotkey to the GNOME keyboard shortcuts. Before you save a combination, the hotkey recorder says that the desktop sets the shortcut, not WhisPaste. On GNOME, “Change in system settings” opens WhisPaste’s page in GNOME Settings, where you change the shortcut.",
  "@manualC14S15H1": {"description": "Handbook: hotkey under Wayland. {k1} = the button label “Add to GNOME”, {k2} = settings section name.", "placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S15H2": "On other desktops, such as Sway or Hyprland, assign the command to a key combination yourself. Settings shows the exact command for your installation under {k1}.",
  "@manualC14S15H2": {"description": "Handbook: manual shortcut on other Wayland desktops. {k1} = settings section name.", "placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S15H3": "If the hotkey does not work in other apps, Settings and onboarding show a notice with the reason, the command for your own key combination, and a “Reconnect” button that asks the desktop again.",
  "@manualC14S15H3": {"description": "Handbook: what the Wayland hotkey notice offers. “{r}” = the Reconnect button label."},
  "manualC14S15H4": "On first start the AppImage adds an entry to the application menu. You can start WhisPaste from there, and the system settings find the app, keyboard shortcuts included. If you move the file, WhisPaste updates the entry on the next start. If you delete the AppImage, the entry stays behind; remove ~/.local/share/applications/de.whispaste.app.desktop by hand.",
  "manualC14S15Heading": "Auto-Paste on Linux",
  "@manualC14S15Heading": {
    "description": "Heading of handbook chapter 14, section 15: auto-paste on Linux."
  },
  "manualC14S15HotkeyHeading": "Hotkey under Wayland",
  "manualC14S15InstallExtCmd": "bash install-gnome-extension.sh",
  "@manualC14S15InstallExtCmd": {"description": "Terminal command, not translated."},
  "manualC14S15SnapCmd": "sudo snap connect whispaste:uinput",
  "@manualC14S15SnapCmd": {
    "description": "Shell command for the Snap build. Keep the command unchanged."
  },
  "manualC14S15SnapHeading": "Snap",
  "@manualC14S15SnapHeading": {
    "description": "Subheading for the Snap build, a Linux app package format. Keep the name as is."
  },
  "manualC14S15T1": "On Linux, terminals paste with Ctrl+Shift+V. WhisPaste recognises common terminals and uses that shortcut there on its own: under X11 and XWayland, and on KDE Plasma, Sway, Hyprland, niri, labwc, Wayfire and other wlroots desktops.",
  "@manualC14S15T1": {"description": "Handbook: Linux terminals paste with Ctrl+Shift+V; where WhisPaste recognises them on its own."},
  "manualC14S15T2": "Under GNOME with Wayland, WhisPaste identifies the target app only with the optional WhisPaste extension for GNOME. Without it, WhisPaste does not recognise terminals, and a block list only copies the text.",
  "@manualC14S15T2": {"description": "Handbook: GNOME Wayland needs the optional WhisPaste GNOME extension to identify the target app."},
  "manualC14S15T3": "If a terminal still shows ^V instead of the text, turn on “{k1}” under {k2}. WhisPaste then pastes with Ctrl+Shift+V everywhere. Some apps use these keys for something else; LibreOffice, for example, opens a dialog.",
  "@manualC14S15T3": {"description": "Handbook: fallback switch. {k1} = the switch title, {k2} = the settings section name.", "placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S15T4": "Under GNOME, turn on the WhisPaste extension in the settings with {k1}. The .deb package already installs it. With Flatpak, AppImage or the tarball, install it once with the script from the unpacked Linux package, then turn it on:",
  "@manualC14S15T4": {"description": "Handbook: how to install and turn on the GNOME extension. {k1} = the button label.", "placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S15T5": "Without uinput set up, for example in Flatpak, WhisPaste pastes through desktop permission: {k1} under {k2}. Your desktop asks once; you can withdraw the permission there at any time",
  "@manualC14S15T5": {"description": "Handbook: portal paste without uinput. {k1} = button label, {k2} = settings section name.", "placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S15TerminalHeading": "Terminals and Wayland",
  "@manualC14S15TerminalHeading": {"description": "Handbook troubleshooting heading: terminal paste shortcut under Wayland."},
  "manualC14S16B2": "What to do: choose another combination under {k1}. On the Mac a tap on Fn opens the emoji picker, and two taps may start system dictation. To avoid that, set \"Press 🌐 key to\" to \"Do Nothing\" in System Settings → Keyboard.",
  "@manualC14S16B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S16B3": "WhisPaste warns about a combination the system already uses. You can still save; the warning names the system function and the next step.",
  "manualC14S16B4": "If WhisPaste reports an unknown key, no other app is the cause: the saved key can't be used as a hotkey, for example after an import. Pick a different combination in that case too. Function keys work from F1 to F20.",
  "@manualC14S16B4": {"description": "Manual: what an unknown-key message means."},
  "manualC14S16Heading": "Hotkey already in use",
  "manualC14S17B2": "What to do: check the autostart permissions in your operating system. Without autostart, WhisPaste does not start on its own.",
  "manualC14S17Heading": "Autostart not set up",
  "manualC14S18B2": "What to do: choose a different, reachable folder under {k1}.",
  "@manualC14S18B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S18Heading": "Automatic backup failed",
  "manualC14S19B2": "What to do: choose “Try again”, or restart WhisPaste and run the reset again afterwards.",
  "manualC14S19Heading": "Factory reset incomplete",
  "manualC14S1B1": "Each section starts with a notice WhisPaste shows. The wording is the app’s own text. Below it are the cause and the next step.",
  "manualC14S1Heading": "How to read this page",
  "manualC14S20B2": "What to do: check the microphone under {k1} and then record again.",
  "@manualC14S20B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S20B3": "If WhisPaste suggests another microphone, the notice shows “Suggestion: device” and a “Use” button. It only switches when you click it.",
  "manualC14S20Heading": "No sound detected",
  "manualC14S21B1": "WhisPaste still starts with less memory than the recommended {k1}. The app then recommends the “Fast” speech model.",
  "@manualC14S21B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S21Heading": "Not enough memory",
  "manualC14S22B1": "Sometimes the system drops the global hotkey, for example after sleep or when the computer was very busy. WhisPaste registers it again on its own: after the computer wakes up, after the speech model was unloaded to free memory, after a long freeze of the window and every five minutes. It does not do this while you dictate or hold the key. If the hotkey still does not respond, restart WhisPaste. A notice appears only after three failed attempts in a row.",
  "manualC14S22B2": "If another app uses the same combination, pick a different one under {k1}.",
  "@manualC14S22B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S22Heading": "The hotkey stopped responding",
  "manualC14S23B1": "Above the field there are three rows: speech recognition, microphone and pasting. If something is missing, the row has exactly one button that takes you to the right step or loads the speech model. While the speech model is loading, the field unlocks by itself. If pasting says \"off\" or \"via clipboard only\", you can still try it out: the text lands in the field, and in other apps you paste it yourself.",
  "manualC14S23Heading": "\"Try it\" shows what is still missing",
  "manualC14S2B2": "What to do: on macOS and Windows, allow WhisPaste microphone access via “Open System Settings”; on Linux, check the microphone in the sound settings. Then start the recording again.",
  "manualC14S2Heading": "No microphone access",
  "manualC14S3B2": "What to do: connect a microphone and choose it under {k1}.",
  "@manualC14S3B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S3Heading": "No microphone found",
  "manualC14S4B2": "What to do: check that the right microphone is selected and not muted in the system settings. If your recordings sound too quiet, turn on “{k1}”. Then record again. With the MacBook lid closed the built-in microphone has no signal: WhisPaste neither forces nor suggests it. If the built-in microphone stayed silent, the next recording uses another device and tells you which.",
  "@manualC14S4B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S4Heading": "No audio captured",
  "manualC14S5B2": "What to do: download a model under {k1}, then try again.",
  "@manualC14S5B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S5Heading": "No speech model",
  "manualC14S6B2": "What to do: choose an installed model under {k1}.",
  "@manualC14S6B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S6Heading": "Unknown model",
  "manualC14S7B2": "What to do: try again. If that does not help, restart WhisPaste or choose another model under {k1}.",
  "@manualC14S7B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC14S7Heading": "Transcription failed",
  "manualC14S8B2": "What to do: speak more clearly or a little longer and try again. Also check the microphone.",
  "manualC14S8Heading": "No text recognized",
  "manualC14S9B2": "What to do: record shorter sections.",
  "manualC14S9Heading": "Recording or transcription too long",
  "manualC14Title": "Troubleshooting",
  "manualC15S1B1": "These keyboard shortcuts work only while the WhisPaste window is active. The system-wide hotkey, by contrast, is set under {k2}.",
  "@manualC15S1B1": {"placeholders": {"k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S1B2": "On Mac, {k1} usually replaces {k2}.",
  "@manualC15S1B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S1B3I1": "F1 opens help.",
  "@manualC15S1B3I1": {"description": "Shortcut reference: the F1 key opens help."},
  "manualC15S1B3I2": "{k} opens help as well.",
  "@manualC15S1B3I2": {"description": "Shortcut reference: the platform chord {k} also opens help.", "placeholders": {"k": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S1Heading": "Only inside WhisPaste",
  "manualC15S2B1I1": "{k1}: delete the entry.",
  "@manualC15S2B1I1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S2B1I2": "{k1}: toggle favorite.",
  "@manualC15S2B1I2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S2B1I3": "{k1}: search.",
  "@manualC15S2B1I3": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S2B1I4": "{k1}: close search or the detail view.",
  "@manualC15S2B1I4": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S2B1I5": "{k1}: copy the entry.",
  "@manualC15S2B1I5": {"description": "Handbuch Kap. 15, Aufnahmen: Enter copies the focused entry. {k1} is the key name.", "placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S2B1I6": "{k1}: discard an edit. If there are changes, it asks first, and the text from before the edit comes back.",
  "@manualC15S2B1I6": {"description": "Handbuch Kap. 15, Aufnahmen: Esc in the transcript editor discards the edit; unsaved changes ask first. {k1} is the key name.", "placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S2B1I7": "While you edit a recording’s text, WhisPaste saves it by itself a moment after you stop typing, when you leave the field and when you open another entry. The label next to the text shows “Saving…” and “Saved”. If saving fails, it says “Not saved yet” and the text stays in the editor.",
  "@manualC15S2B1I7": {"description": "Handbuch Kap. 15, Aufnahmen: the transcript editor saves automatically and shows its status (#634)."},
  "manualC15S2Heading": "Recordings",
  "manualC15S3B1I1": "{k1}: delete the entry.",
  "@manualC15S3B1I1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S3B1I2": "{k1}: create a new note.",
  "@manualC15S3B1I2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S3B1I3": "{k1}: search.",
  "@manualC15S3B1I3": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S3B1I4": "{k1}: focus mode on and off; Esc leaves it.",
  "@manualC15S3B1I4": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S3Heading": "Notes",
  "manualC15S4B1I1": "{k1}: delete the entry.",
  "@manualC15S4B1I1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S4Heading": "Dictionary and snippets",
  "manualC15S5B1": "While a recording or transcription runs, WhisPaste catches the {k1} key system-wide so you can cancel. {k1} then does nothing in other apps. If that bothers you, choose {k2} as the cancel key in Settings. While no recording runs, {k1} also closes a standing notice card and {k2} presses its first button (30 seconds at most). On the preview of an AI edit, Enter applies the result instead.",
  "@manualC15S5B1": {"description": "Manual paragraph: while a recording or transcription runs, WhisPaste catches the cancel key ({k1}, Esc) system-wide; {k2} (F12) is the alternative to choose in Settings (UIA-SHL-14).", "placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S5Heading": "Cancel during a recording",
  "@manualC15S5Heading": {"description": "Manual heading: cancelling a dictation with Esc, which is caught system-wide while a recording runs (UIA-SHL-14)."},
  "manualC15S6B1": "On the Mac, the most important commands are also in the menu bar at the top. The Recording menu starts and stops a recording and sets the language, speech model, Smart Mode, what happens after transcription, the microphone and the profile.",
  "manualC15S6B2I1": "WhisPaste: open Settings with {k1}",
  "@manualC15S6B2I1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC15S6B2I2": "Edit: Cut, Copy, Paste and Select All act on the text field that has focus.",
  "manualC15S6B2I3": "Window: minimize and zoom the main window.",
  "manualC15S6B2I4": "Help: open the manual, send feedback and suggest an idea.",
  "manualC15S6B3": "On Windows and Linux, the same commands are in a menu button at the top right of the title bar. Press Alt or F10 to open it, use the arrow keys to choose an entry, Enter to run it and Escape to close the menu.",
  "manualC15S6Heading": "Menu",
  "manualC15Title": "Keyboard shortcuts",
  "manualC16S1B1I1": "Rating: required.",
  "manualC16S1B1I2": "Type: required. Choose Problem, Idea, Praise, or Question.",
  "manualC16S1B1I3": "Free text up to 1000 characters.",
  "manualC16S1B1I4": "An email address is optional.",
  "manualC16S1B2": "If you have sent feedback recently, the app shows this notice:",
  "manualC16S1Heading": "Sending feedback",
  "manualC16S2B1": "The {k1} tab on the Help page shows how you can support WhisPaste.",
  "@manualC16S2B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC16S2B2": "The {k1} tab sorts the ways by kind: one-time (PayPal, Ko-fi or Buy Me a Coffee in the direct versions, plus buying in the store), monthly (GitHub Sponsors) and without money (rate in the store of your version, or on GitHub on Linux and with package managers; star on GitHub, tell others). Store versions offer the store's own tip instead of external payment pages; the Mac App Store shows no external links at all. The coffee button also sits under the version in Settings, and the tray menu opens this tab.",
  "@manualC16S2B2": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC16S2B3": "After a few weeks of regular use, a quiet card on the Overview asks for support at most three times, never while you dictate and never as a dialog. {k1} opens the {k4} tab, {k2} asks again in 90 days at the earliest, {k3} ends the ask for good. If you already support WhisPaste or a store payment is pending, you do not see the card.",
  "@manualC16S2B3": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}, "k3": {"type": "String"}, "k4": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC16S2Heading": "Support",
  "manualC16S3B1": "If you chose “Don't ask again” for the rating reminder, you find the switch “{k2}” under {k1}. It appears only in that case.",
  "@manualC16S3B1": {"description": "Handbook paragraph: where the switch that lifts 'Don't ask again' is. {k1} is the Interface area, {k2} the switch label.", "placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC16S3Heading": "Rating reminder",
  "@manualC16S3Heading": {"description": "Handbook heading: rating reminder and how to lift a 'Don't ask again' choice."},
  "manualC16S4B1": "Everything about feedback and contact is on the {k1} Help tab: the form, “Report issue”, ideas and the email address. “Contact us…” in the Help menu of the menu bar (macOS) or the title-bar menu (Windows and Linux) leads there too. “Send feedback…” in the tray menu opens the form directly.",
  "@manualC16S4B1": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualC16S4Heading": "Where to find the form",
  "manualC16S5B1": "Choose Idea, or open Suggest an idea from the Help menu: the type is already set. The best place for your idea is the public ideas board, where others can vote. If you would rather not, write it in this form.",
  "manualC16S5Heading": "Share an idea",
  "manualC16S6B1": "The microphone button in the message field records your voice and inserts the recognized text at the cursor.",
  "manualC16S6Heading": "Write by voice",
  "manualC16S7B1I1": "The type you chose and your message.",
  "manualC16S7B1I2": "The rating you chose.",
  "manualC16S7B1I3": "Your email address only if you gave one. Message and address live only in our team inbox, serve only the reply and are deleted once resolved, after 12 months at the latest.",
  "manualC16S7B1I4": "Diagnostics only if you turn on Attach diagnostics: app version, operating system, speech engine and backend. Show preview displays the exact text before you send.",
  "manualC16S7B1I5": "Only for Praise: the consent to show your praise as a quote on the website. It is off by default and you can withdraw it with one click on this page.",
  "manualC16S7B2": "Recordings and transcripts are never attached.",
  "manualC16S7Heading": "What is sent",
  "manualC16S8B1": "The two cards show the stars on GitHub and the subscribers on YouTube. The app asks the server for the numbers at most once a day, and only while the overview or Help → Support is open. Numbers older than seven days are no longer shown; without a number the card only says what the link is for. Below them, “Support WhisPaste” opens Help → Support, which shows what your version offers; the button is missing when your version offers nothing there. Direct downloads also show “Submit an idea”, store versions “Rate in the store”. The idea button carries the external-link mark and first asks before it opens the ideas board in your browser. Under “From the developer”, two links lead to the developer's profiles on X and GitHub. Hide the section with “Hide this section” or under “Customise overview”.",
  "manualC16S8B2": "Under “Newsletter” you can sign up directly with your email address. First tick the consent box, then click “Sign up”. The app sends the address to the WhisPaste server only at that moment and does not store it itself. You get an email with a confirmation link; you are signed up only once you click it. The link is valid for 48 hours. You can unsubscribe through the link in every newsletter email.",
  "manualC16S8Heading": "Getting involved on the overview",
  "manualC16Title": "Feedback and support",
  "manualCodeCopy": "Copy command",
  "@manualCodeCopy": {
    "description": "Label of the copy button next to a command block, also its tooltip. Use the same wording as pasteFailureLinuxUinputCopy."
  },
  "manualDownloadOfflineAction": "What to do: check your internet connection and retry the download. If this computer stays offline, download the model file on another computer and transfer it. Follow “{k1}” in the “{k2}” chapter.",
  "@manualDownloadOfflineAction": {"placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}, "description": "Troubleshooting: offline download recovery. k1 is the manual installation section heading; k2 is the hardware chapter title."},
  "manualDownloadOfflineCause": "Cause: WhisPaste cannot reach the download server, for example because this computer has no internet connection.",
  "manualDownloadOfflineHeading": "No connection during download",
  "manualLangCycleB1": "In the Dictate card you can assign a key combination that switches the recording language to the next favorite on each press. The shortcut stays off until you record a combination. Without favorites, WhisPaste points you to the setting. Under Linux with Wayland, other apps may not respond to the shortcut.",
  "@manualLangCycleB1": {"description": "Manual paragraph on the next-favorite-language hotkey, including the Wayland limit."},
  "manualLangCycleHeading": "Next favorite language",
  "@manualLangCycleHeading": {"description": "Manual section heading: the next-favorite-language hotkey."},
  "manualLangFavB1": "Mark up to five favorite languages with the star: in Settings under Speech recognition → Recognition language → Change, or in the language menu of the recording dock. Click the star again to remove a favorite. In the selection dialog the favorites sit at the top in a group of their own. They keep the order you starred them in; a new favorite goes to the end. To move a language to the end, remove its star and set it again. Favorites appear first, in this order, in the language menu of the recording dock and in the tray menu, and the “Next favorite language” hotkey steps through them in the same order.",
  "@manualLangFavB1": {"description": "Manual paragraph explaining the up-to-five favorite languages: set by star, their order, and where they appear."},
  "manualLangFavHeading": "Favorite languages",
  "@manualLangFavHeading": {"description": "Manual section heading: favorite speech languages."},
  "manualLangTrayB1": "The tray menu and the floating button's menu have a Language submenu. It lists your favorites in their order and puts a check mark on the active language. More languages… opens the full language selection in Settings. Parakeet picks its own language, so the submenu is hidden there.",
  "@manualLangTrayB1": {"description": "Manual paragraph on the tray menu Language submenu."},
  "manualLangTrayHeading": "Language in the tray menu",
  "@manualLangTrayHeading": {"description": "Manual section heading: the Language submenu in the tray menu."},
  "manualNoticeLabel": "Message shown in the app",
  "manualProfilesS1B1": "Speech model, language, Smart Mode, after-transcription action and microphone",
  "manualProfilesS1B2": "Up to 9 profiles. Every field can be set to “Leave unchanged”.",
  "manualProfilesS1Body": "A profile is a named set of recording settings. Create it under Settings → Profiles and decide for each field whether the profile sets it or leaves it unchanged.",
  "manualProfilesS1Heading": "Creating profiles",
  "manualProfilesS2Body": "Each profile can have its own hotkey. Pressing it activates the profile, and it stays active until you choose another one or change one of its fields by hand.",
  "manualProfilesS2Heading": "Hotkeys",
  "manualProfilesS2Tip": "A hotkey needs a modifier key. A combination that another feature already uses stays inactive, and the profile shows it as a conflict.",
  "manualProfilesS3Body": "If you add an app under target apps, the profile becomes active whenever that app is in front while you dictate. The identifier depends on the operating system:",
  "manualProfilesS3Heading": "Automatic per app",
  "manualProfilesS3Linux": "Linux: window WM_CLASS under X11, for example google-chrome. Under Wayland WhisPaste cannot detect the app.",
  "manualProfilesS3Mac": "macOS: app identifier, for example com.apple.mail",
  "manualProfilesS3Recent": "It is easier with “Recently dictated into”: it lists the apps you dictated into most recently, with name and icon. One click assigns the app to the profile. Apps that already belong to a profile, and WhisPaste itself, are left out. For apps you have not dictated into, the identifier and “Capture app” remain.",
  "manualProfilesS3Win": "Windows: program name without .exe, for example chrome",
  "manualProfilesS4Body": "The tray menu has a Profile submenu: “Automatic, by app” follows the rules, “No profile” switches them off, and your profiles are listed below. In the recording overlay, the name of the active profile follows the time and language, for example 0:07 · DE · Mail.",
  "manualProfilesS4Heading": "Tray and overlay",
  "manualProfilesS5Body": "“Leave unchanged” means the profile does not touch this field. The value currently set in Settings applies.",
  "manualProfilesS5Heading": "Leave unchanged",
  "manualProfilesS6Body": "If you choose a profile in the tray or press its hotkey, it applies to all following recordings. If you then change a field the profile sets, such as the speech model, the active marker ends and the per-app rules decide again. The choice survives a restart.",
  "manualProfilesS6Heading": "What a manual change does",
  "manualProfilesTitle": "Profiles and quick switching",
  "manualSearchEmpty": "No chapter matches this search.",
  "manualSearchHint": "Search the handbook",
  "manualSearchResultCount": "{count, plural, =0{No chapters found} =1{1 chapter found} other{{count} chapters found}}",
  "@manualSearchResultCount": {"description": "Live status under the handbook search: how many chapters match.", "placeholders": {"count": {"type": "int"}}},
  "manualSoundB1": "{k1} sets the character of all sounds. Below it you switch each sound on or off: start, stop, done, warning, error and paste. The paste sound is off by default. The play button next to it previews the sound in the current profile and at the current volume. Sounds the selected profile does not have are greyed out. If {k2} is at 0, everything is silent, but your selection is kept.",
  "@manualSoundB1": {"description": "Manual paragraph on per-cue sound switches. k1 = sound profile label, k2 = sound volume label.", "placeholders": {"k1": {"type": "String"}, "k2": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualSoundHeading": "Choosing individual sounds",
  "@manualSoundHeading": {"description": "Manual section heading: switching single sound cues on or off (#1154)."},
  "manualViewMemoryB1": "Recordings, notes and snippets open where you left them: same cursor or selection, same scroll position and the same text size, also after a restart. Focus goes straight into the text and screen readers announce the paragraph. {zoomIn} and {zoomOut} change the text size of the open entry only, {zoomReset} sets it back to 100%. This is stored beside the text and never changes it; deleting an entry deletes it too.",
  "@manualViewMemoryB1": {"description": "Manual, shortcuts chapter: per-entry cursor, scroll and text size memory (#678). Placeholders are the key combinations.", "placeholders": {"zoomIn": {"type": "String"}, "zoomOut": {"type": "String"}, "zoomReset": {"type": "String"}}},
  "manualViewMemoryHeading": "Where you left off",
  "@manualViewMemoryHeading": {"description": "Manual, shortcuts chapter: heading of the section on reopening entries where they were left (#678)."},
